TITLE: [GNR A0 PO][SRF A0 PO][Security] Linux Kernel boot not aware of TME AES-XTS-256 - x86/tme: Unknown policy is active & x86/mktme: No known encryption algorithm is supported

DESCRIPTION:
Summary: Linux Kernel seems to only be aware of TME_ACTIVATE_POLICY_AES_XTS_128 and GNR is using AES-XTS-256 for MKTME by default. Linux boots with pr_err and ultimately leaves mktme_status = MKTME_DISABLED; Description: Planned RAS tests involving TME encountered the following during enabling, which seems to be cosmetic but we still need this sorted out. root@JF530NOR16BN0202T ~]# dmesg | grep tme [ 2.177389] Initmem setup node 0 [mem 0x0000000000001000-0x000000207fffffff] [ 4.606806] rcu: RCU calculated value of scheduler-enlistment delay is 100 jiffies. [ 4.843342] x86/tme: enabled by BIOS [ 4.844332] x86/tme: Unknown policy is active: 0x2 [ 4.845333] x86/mktme: No known encryption algorithm is supported: 0x4 [ 4.846335] x86/mktme: enabled by BIOS [ 4.847332] x86/mktme: 63 KeyIDs available [ 34.376046] Freeing unused kernel image (initmem) memory: 2932K Which comes from /arch/x86/kernel/cpu/intel.c . tme_policy = TME_ACTIVATE_POLICY(tme_activate);
 if (tme_policy != TME_ACTIVATE_POLICY_AES_XTS_128)
 pr_warn(&quot;x86/tme: Unknown policy is active: %#llx\n&quot;, tme_policy); 

 tme_crypto_algs = TME_ACTIVATE_CRYPTO_ALGS(tme_activate);
 if (!(tme_crypto_algs & TME_ACTIVATE_CRYPTO_AES_XTS_128)) {
 pr_err(&quot;x86/mktme: No known encryption algorithm is supported: %#llx\n&quot;, 
 tme_crypto_algs); 
 mktme_status = MKTME_DISABLED; 
 } This was seen in Intel-next 5.19.0-gnr.po.bkc.1.1.2.x86_64 and also present in upstream with a code inspection from /arch/x86/kernel/cpu/intel.c . The sillicon has the following setting [root@JF530NOR16BN0202T fisher]# rdmsr -0 0x982
0x000400060000002b
 :
In [152]: sv.sockets.soc.memss.mc0.ch0.mse.mse_activate.mse_policy.getspec()
##########################################################################################################################
# FIELD: mse_policy
# Attribute: rw
# UpperBit: 7
# LowerBit: 4
# CurrentValue: 2 
# Description:
# 0000 - AES_XTS-128, 0001 - AES_XTS-128 with Integrity, 0010 - AES-XTS-256 , 0011 - AES_XTS-256 with Integrity

COMMENTS:
Looking at the BIOS knobs kernel is reflecting what kernel is printing. TME enabled by BIOS: MKTME enabled by BIOS: And AES-XTS-256 algorithm enabled by default: Looks like the issue here is that the kernel doesn't know what to do when AES-XTS-256 algorithm is enabled
Booting with AES-XTS-128 algorithm system is not landing in to the errors seen with AES-XTS-256 [root@JF530NOR16BN0202T ~]# dmesg | grep tme [    2.177690] Initmem setup node 0 [mem 0x0000000000001000-0x000000207fffffff] [    4.605298] rcu: RCU calculated value of scheduler-enlistment delay is 100 jiffies. [    4.842911] x86/tme: enabled by BIOS [    4.843900] x86/mktme: enabled by BIOS [    4.844900] x86/mktme: 63 KeyIDs available [   34.401801] Freeing unused kernel image (initmem) memory: 2932K Adding @Chen, Tim C and @Sun, Yannan to help us getting more inputs from kernel and security sides.
From MSR perspective, the value is correct. 0x4 means the crypo algorithm is AES-256.   @Chen, Tim C The default cryptographic algorithm for MKTME is AES-128 w/o integrity on SPR and has been changed to AES-256 w/o integrity on GNR (FR: https://hsdes.intel.com/appstore/article/#/14017005550) Looks like there is some gap in the implementation. Run itp.threads[0].msr(0x982) [64b] 0x000100060000000B BHS note: [64b] 0x000400060000002B Bit [0] should be set, indicating MSR 0x982 latched. Bit [1] should be set, indicating TME enabled. Bits [7:4] should be 0x2, indicating crypto algorithm is AES_XTS_256 (this is the default option if usrs did not set). Bits [51:48] should be 0x4, indicating crypto algorithm is AES_XTS_256 without integrity (this is the default option if uses did not set).
Another question, how does kernel handle TDX with respect to the dependency on MKTME? MKTME enablement is a pre-condition for TDX enabling, TDX works fine right now. Not sure if TDX enabling in kernel is based on the value of MSR 0x982 or the mktme_status in the code in description.
Checked with our TME developer Alison Schofield.  The message is purely cosmetic and informational and does not affect TME operation. There are plan in place to remove the warning.  And mktme_status is not used anywhere in the kernel.
Hao, can you answer Yannan's question " how does kernel handle TDX with respect to the dependency on MKTME? MKTME enablement is a pre-condition for TDX enabling"
Kernel tdx code will check MSR_IA32_MKTME_KEYID_PARTITIONING (0x00000087).
JIRA ticket to track this issue in Linux database is created: https://jira.devtools.intel.com/browse/LFE-6447
How to verify the message will not affect actual TME behavior with AES-256? Once confirmed, we would lower the priority.
You can do a grep of mktme_status in kernel code and see that it is not checked by any other code in Linux kernel.  Also confirmed by Hao that the key kernel component TDX depending on SGX does not use this but check msr directly.  There is work in progress by Alison to clean up and remove mktme_status entirely for upstream kernel.
ok thanks Tim, Keep open to track the cleanup, lower to P4.
Low priority, let linux team track it in JIRA.
Low priority, but we are still wanting this get fixed.
@Avelar, Omar @Wanyan, Feiting Are you still tracking this issue? I don't have access to the JIRA. So pls. update if there's plan to address this issue from kernel team.
One of the obstacles in posting a fix for this upstream is testing it. Please let me know when a GNR system, demonstrating the problem, is available for loan.
Hi RAS team - Is the issue still relevant with 6.6 GNR/SRF kernels? Can anyone reproduce the issue and share the system with Alison as requested below? thank you Shashi Kiran [SRF Platform Sysdebug]
@Yang, Shitao Please help check if still reproducible with v6.6 kernels. And share Alison a system if needed. Thanks~
The issue is reproducible in upstream and BKC since there is no change done yet in the relevant code @ https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/intel.c What @Schofield, Alison needs is a system either GNR or SRF that boots TME AES-XTS-256, please lend her one
It is reproduced with v6.6 kernels. [root@gnr-bkc ~]# dmesg | grep tme [    0.026164] Initmem setup node 0 [mem 0x0000000000001000-0x000000407effffff] [    0.026171] Initmem setup node 1 [mem 0x0000004080000000-0x000000807fffffff] [    0.026175] Initmem setup node 2 [mem 0x000000c080000000-0x000001007fffffff] [    0.026182] Initmem setup node 3 [mem 0x0000010080000000-0x000001407fffffff] [    0.026297] Initmem setup node 4 as memoryless [    0.026410] Initmem setup node 5 as memoryless [    0.026518] Initmem setup node 6 as memoryless [    0.026624] Initmem setup node 7 as memoryless [   20.287410] rcu: RCU calculated value of scheduler-enlistment delay is 100 jiffies. [   20.343363] x86/tme: enabled by BIOS [   20.343364] x86/tme: Unknown policy is active: 0x2 [   20.343367] x86/mktme: No known encryption algorithm is supported: 0x4 [   20.344355] x86/mktme: enabled by BIOS [   20.344356] x86/mktme: 127 KeyIDs available [   31.073524] Freeing unused kernel image (initmem) memory: 4848K [root@gnr-bkc ~]# uname -a Linux gnr-bkc 6.6.0-gnr.bkc.6.6.12.6.18.x86_64 #1 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Thu Jan 18 22:58:27 UTC 2024 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux
@Yang, Shitao I took a look at the system you offered and it is not exhibiting the problem. fl31ca103dn0503.amr.corp.intel.com I suspect it is not the system you posted the dmesg log w mktme failures.  I need the directions for turning MKTME on/off in BIOS settings when rebooting.  Please turn it 'on' and capture the screenshots of how you find the settings. I can use that as a reference when I need to turn it off/on again.  I'll start with it 'on'. Thanks!
@Schofield, Alison 0503 must be changed by someone. Please login: fl31ca103cn0106.amr.corp.intel.com TmeCryptoAlg default is C, do noy need to change it. Only set EnableTme=1,EnableMktme=1 is enough. Anything you need, let me know.
@Yang, Shitao  Added this note to our teams chat. Maybe someone else can answer sooner, since you and I seem to be in opposing timezones. Seems to have hung on reboot. I'm looking for QAP tool/screen to power cycle the system.  I don't see it. Is there another way to do that on this system.  If you get a chance to get on - please try to boot my kernel - it's labelled "ALISON" and is set as curr kernel via grub.
