COMMENTS:
@Pan, Lijun , can kernel side check this issue? seems some service starts during booting cause this issue.
Promote to p2 as it is found in multiple systems. Suspect this issue is related to hugepage or TDVMs changes https://github.com/intel-sandbox/os.linux.graniterapids.poweron/blob/06350fbb02b3e3a7520b99d1a82e949766e2985f/mm/vmalloc.c#L3195 https://wiki.ith.intel.com/display/linuxstack/Release+Notes+2024+WW51+GNR
Can you be more specific about the hugepage or TDVMs changes?
Hi @Pan, Lijun , Sorry, I misunderstood the release note. Please comment from your perspective. What I can confirm is that we did not see this issue when 6.6-31, but we hit this in 6.6-32.  We reproduce this on multiple systems and multiple times. It is a regression issue
The only changes in between the gnr-6.6-v31.1 and gnr-6.6-v32.1 is the uncore change, which does not change the memory related codes. I also discussed with Kan Liang, owner of uncore, it seems unlikely to have those uncore patches impacting memory allocation.    Kan said, " There should be some differences in the configuration between 173 and 174. At least, in the 174 log, I saw some hugepages setting. There is no such thing in 173 log.  The uncore change should not impact the vmalloc. I'm not sure if the hugepage settings impact it. One test would be to reduce the buffer which assigned to the hugepage and see if it helps." Please make sure settings are consistent over testing and try reducing the buffer size.
@Pan, Lijun  our script does not perform any hugepage setting, just doing simply cycling test can reproduce it I got the following output for the huge page setting, is it as expected? [root@gnr-bkc ~]# grep HugePages_ /proc/meminfo HugePages_Total:       0 HugePages_Free:        0 HugePages_Rsvd:        0 HugePages_Surp:        0 Do you have any other commands for checking the huge page buffer and reducing the buffer size
